This shifts the focus from a specific relationship to a universal ethical critique of deception. To argue that any lie is an interference with free will, we must define free will as the ability to navigate the world based on genuine data.
When you lie to someone—even a "white lie"—you are effectively "hacking" their decision-making process. You are giving them a broken compass and then judging them for where they walk.
Thesis: The Ontological Theft of the Lie
> "A lie is a form of informational coercion that violates a person’s autonomy by forcing them to live in a fabricated reality. Because human agency relies on the ability to respond to the truth, any intentional deception—regardless of its perceived 'whiteness'—is an act of interference that replaces the victim’s free will with the liar’s intent."
> 
I. The Epistemic Requirement for Agency
For a choice to be "free," the person making it must be the "author" of that choice. Authorship requires a correct understanding of the variables involved.
 * Claim: Truth is the "raw material" of the will.
 * Evidence 1 (Kant’s Categorical Imperative): Immanuel Kant argued that lying is always wrong because it treats the listener as a "thing" to be manipulated, not a person with their own goals. By lying, you rob them of the chance to react to the actual world.
 * Evidence 2 (Thomas Aquinas): Aquinas argued that the "will" is a rational appetite. If the "reason" (the information) is false, the "appetite" (the choice) is directed at a ghost, not a reality.
 * Evidence 3 (Modern Information Theory): In systems theory, "noise" (false data) degrades the quality of the output. A lie is intentional "noise" injected into a person's biological decision-making system.
II. The "White Lie" as Micro-Manipulation
The term "white lie" implies that the harm is zero. However, from the standpoint of Free Will, the harm is the theft of a person's time and response.
 * Forced Consent: If you tell a friend a party is "casual" when it is "formal," they have "freely chosen" their outfit, but that choice was a trap. Their will was steered by your lie.
 * The Sabotage of Self-Correction: We learn by making mistakes based on reality. If someone lies to "protect" us, they steal our opportunity to learn and adapt. They are "pruning" our growth to suit their comfort.
III. The Dialectical Argument (Pro vs. Con)
The Argument FOR (Lying as Interference):
Every lie is a "micro-robbery." It steals the listener’s right to feel what they would naturally feel and do what they would naturally do. If I am "happy" because of a lie, that happiness is not mine; it belongs to the liar who manufactured it. My free will has been replaced by their "script."
The Argument AGAINST (The "Social Harmony" Defense):
A critic might argue that "total honesty" would destroy society. They argue that "white lies" (e.g., "I love your gift") are a form of altruism that lubricates social friction. They claim that "protecting" someone's feelings is more important than their "epistemic accuracy."
The Rebuttal (The Autonomy Defense):
This defense is paternalistic. It assumes that the liar is "smarter" or "stronger" than the listener and can decide what the listener is "ready" to hear. By deciding for the listener, the liar has already ended the listener's free will in that moment. You cannot respect someone while simultaneously deciding they cannot handle the truth.
IV. Conclusion: The Integrity of the Witness
This is why your specific situation with your friend is so heavy. By knowing the lie, you see the "interference" in real-time. You see a person (the non-drinker) living in a simulation designed by the perpetrator.
The moral duty to tell the truth isn't about being "mean" or "causing trouble"; it is about returning the person to the real world. It is an act of liberation.
